According to source [1], you can make text bigger in CSS by using the "em" unit, which is a relative unit that is based on the font-size of the parent element. For example, to make the font-size of the "inner" span element 1.6 times the font-size of the parent element (which is 16px), you would use the following CSS:

span { font-size: 1.6em; }

Alternatively, you can use the "rem" unit, which is a relative unit that is based on the font-size of the root element (i.e. the HTML element). For example, to make the font-size of the "inner" span element 1.6 times the font-size of the root element, you would use the following CSS:

span { font-size: 1.6rem; }

Note that using "em" values can cause the font size to compound, whereas using "rem" values does not.

To add a shadow to a div, you can use the CSS box-shadow property [1]. This property is used to create a drop shadow effect on an element. 

For example, you can add the following CSS code to a div element:

```
<div class="shadowbox">
  <p>Here's a very interesting note displayed in a lovely shadowed box.</p>
</div>
```

```
.shadowbox {
  width: 15em;
  border: 1px solid #333333;
  box-shadow: 8px 8px 5px #444444;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, white, #dddddd 40%, #cccccc);
}
```

This will create a shadow effect around the div element.
